n-square positivity, and integral structure - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2608.29757v2)\nciteturn9search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; New results include a proof of Shareshian and Sundaram's Ramanujan-square conjecture, the integral character lattice, and polyhedral properties of the Schur cone ... We give a unified proof of four conjectures of Sundaram on Schur positivity by classifying all Schur-positive Boolean $Q$-sums; the case of sums over divisors up to a prescribed bound recovers Hou's theorem. ... We compute its Ehrhart polynomial and volume, prove its integer decomposition property, and determine the Hilbert basis of its cone.\nA Boolean $Q$-sum is a sum of distinct elements of this basis. We give a unified proof of four conjectures of Sundaram on Schur positivity by classifying all Schur-positive Boolean $Q$-sums; the case of sums over divisors up to a prescribed bound recovers Hou's theorem. Specifically, for a nonempty set $J$ of divisors of $n$, the sum $\\sum_{d\\in J}Q_{n,d}$ is Schur-positive exactly when $1\\in J$ and, for even $n$, $n\\in J$ implies $n/2\\in J$. The same character estimates prove the Ramanujan-square conjecture of Shareshian and Sundaram: the function $\\sum_{d\\mid n}c_d(n/d)^2p_d^{n/d}$, where $c_d(r)$ is the Ramanujan sum, has a positive coefficient of $s_λ$ for every $n\\ge1$ and $λ\\vdash n$, except when $n\\equiv2\\pmod4$ and $λ=(1^n)$, in which case the coefficient is zero. We prove that an element of this space has integral Schur coefficients if and only if its $Q$-coordinates are integral. The Boolean classification also determines the convex hull of the Schur-positive Boolean points with $Q_{n,1}$-coordinate $1$. We compute its Ehrhart polynomial and volume, prove its integer decomposition property, and determine the Hilbert basis of its cone. For $n\\ge18$, we prove that setting the coefficient of $s_{(n)}$ or $s_{(1^n)}$ equal to $0$ or $1$ defines a facet of the section of the Schur-positive cone with $Q_{n,1}$-coordinate $1$. The positivity results and coordinate formulas also yield inequalities for major-index residue multiplicities.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nschur positivity — arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/keyword/schur%20positivity)\nciteturn9search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Motivated by questions of Schur positivity, we provide a combinatorial description of crystal skeletons, and prove many new properties, including a conjecture by Maas-Gariépy that crystal skeletons generalize dual equivalence graphs. ... We give a unified proof of four conjectures of Sundaram on Schur positivity by classifying all Schur-positive Boolean $Q$-sums; the case of sums over divisors up to a prescribed bound recovers Hou's theorem. ... We compute its Ehrhart polynomial and volume, prove its integer decomposition property, and determine the Hilbert basis of its cone.\nA Boolean $Q$-sum is a sum of distinct elements of this basis. We give a unified proof of four conjectures of Sundaram on Schur positivity by classifying all Schur-positive Boolean $Q$-sums; the case of sums over divisors up to a prescribed bound recovers Hou's theorem. Specifically, for a nonempty set $J$ of divisors of $n$, the sum $\\sum_{d\\in J}Q_{n,d}$ is Schur-positive exactly when $1\\in J$ and, for even $n$, $n\\in J$ implies $n/2\\in J$. The same character estimates prove the Ramanujan-square conjecture of Shareshian and Sundaram: the function $\\sum_{d\\mid n}c_d(n/d)^2p_d^{n/d}$, where $c_d(r)$ is the Ramanujan sum, has a positive coefficient of $s_λ$ for every $n\\ge1$ and $λ\\vdash n$, except when $n\\equiv2\\pmod4$ and $λ=(1^n)$, in which case the coefficient is zero. We prove that an element of this space has integral Schur coefficients if and only if its $Q$-coordinates are integral. The Boolean classification also determines the convex hull of the Schur-positive Boolean points with $Q_{n,1}$-coordinate $1$. We compute its Ehrhart polynomial and volume, prove its integer decomposition property, and determine the Hilbert basis of its cone. For $n\\ge18$, we prove that setting the coefficient of $s_{(n)}$ or $s_{(1^n)}$ equal to $0$ or $1$ defines a facet of the section of the Schur-positive cone with $Q_{n,1}$-coordinate $1$. The positivity results and coordinate formulas also yield inequalities for major-index residue multiplicities.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChromatic quasisymmetric functions | SymCat (https://www.symmetricfunctions.com/chromaticQuasisymmetric.htm)\nciteturn9search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Gasharov proved that the \\(\\chrom_G(\\xvec;1)\\) are Schur-positive with an explicit expansion, whenever \\(G\\) is the incomparability graph of a \\(3+1\\)-free poset. ... Siegl†scholar.google.com】 gives another proof of this Schur positivity and interprets the coefficients by nonintersecting paths. ... This conjecture has been verified for all circular unit arc digraphs up to 10 vertices.\n### #Schur positivity\n\nIn [Gas96], V. Gasharov proved that the \\(\\chrom_G(\\xvec;1)\\) are Schur-positive with an explicit expansion, whenever \\(G\\) is the incomparability graph of a \\(3+1\\)-free poset. In particular, this implies the unit-interval case, as those are given as the incomparability graphs of \\(3+1\\) and \\(2+2\\)-free posets. Gasharov’s proof uses a kind of sign-reversing involution.\n\nGasharov’s result was later extended to the Shareshian–Wachs setting (with a \\(q\\)-parameter), by using a different involution. That is, \\(\\chrom_G(\\xvec;q)\\) is Schur-positive for unit-interval graphs \\(G,\\) see [SW12, Thm. 6.3]. D. Kim and P. Pylyavskyy give a Robinson–Schensted correspondence for unit interval orders which refines the Schur expansion through dual equivalence graphs [KP21]. Using a directed-network model for multivari